---
/**
 * Chapter 4 — the detection limit for event effects.
 *
 * The subject of this chapter is the method, not the government. Three events
 * are tested; none produces an effect distinguishable from what the same
 * procedure finds in years when nothing happened. That is a statement about
 * how large a signal this data can resolve, and it is written that way
 * throughout — see PLAN.md's drafting note.
 *
 * The chart is the argument: each event's estimate is drawn inside the cloud
 * of its own placebos. Without that cloud, -0.96 µg/m³ looks like a finding.
 */
import detection from "../../public/data/story/detection-limit.json";
import { linear, n, ticks } from "../lib/chart";
import { href } from "../lib/chapters";
import ChapterIntro from "./ChapterIntro.astro";
import EvidenceFigure from "./EvidenceFigure.astro";

const events = detection.events as {
  event: string;
  kind: string;
  n_stations: number;
  median_effect: number;
  median_placebo_mean: number;
  median_placebo_sd: number;
  n_credible: number;
  n_expected_by_chance: number;
  credible_stations: string[];
  placebo_effects: number[];
  station_effects: { station: string; effect: number }[];
}[];

const spatial = detection.spatial_check as {
  label: string;
  why: string;
  stations: { station: string; effect: number; z: number }[];
};

const windowed = events.filter((e) => e.kind === "window");
const breaks = events.filter((e) => e.kind === "trend_break");

/*
 * One strip per event: placebos as a faint rug, the real estimates stacked
 * under it.
 *
 * The real estimates used to be 73 dots on ONE line at `top: 70%`. 73 marks 7px
 * across need 511px of axis and the strip is nowhere near that wide, so they
 * overlapped into a single unbroken bar — the caption said 「73 個亮點」 and the
 * figure showed one. A row of coincident dots cannot show a distribution, which
 * is the only thing this figure is for.
 *
 * So they are binned and stacked: a Wilkinson dot plot. Each column is one bin,
 * each dot in it one station, and the height of the column IS the count. The
 * placebo cloud keeps its own encoding — overlapping hairlines at low alpha,
 * where the compositing is the density — because it has 1,295 members and
 * stacking those would be a histogram of a different figure.
 */
const DOT = 7;

/*
 * One frame for every strip, centred on zero.
 *
 * The strips are stacked directly on top of each other, which is a promise that
 * they can be compared — and they could not be. Each event solved its own
 * domain from its own placebo range, so measured at 1440: frame spans of 21.33
 * and 28.83 μg/m³ across the same 903px, zero at 58.1% of one and 50.5% of the
 * other, **69px apart on the page**. A reader lining the two up was reading a
 * difference in scaling as a difference in effect, and the wider placebo cloud
 * — which is the chapter's whole subject — was drawn exactly as wide as the
 * narrower one.
 *
 * The old comment here argued the domains must not be touched because "the two
 * events genuinely have different ranges and forcing a shared frame would
 * compress one of them". They do, and it does: the COVID strip's data now
 * occupies about two thirds of the width instead of all of it. That is the
 * price of the two figures meaning the same thing, and it is the smaller loss.
 *
 * Symmetric, so zero lands at exactly 50% in both and the eye can find it
 * without a label.
 */
const FRAME = (() => {
  const all = windowed.flatMap((e) =>
    e.placebo_effects.concat(e.station_effects.map((s) => s.effect)),
  );
  const reach = Math.max(1, ...all.map((v) => Math.abs(v)));
  // The same 5% breathing room the per-event frames had, on a symmetric span.
  const pad = reach * 0.1;
  return { lo: -(reach + pad), hi: reach + pad };
})();

/*
 * One bin is the same width in DATA units in every strip, so a column of five
 * dots means the same thing in both. Bins were a fixed count across each
 * event's own frame, which made the bin 0.38 μg/m³ wide in one strip and 0.51
 * in the other — the taller column was partly just the coarser bin.
 */
const BIN = 0.38;
const BINS = Math.max(8, Math.round((FRAME.hi - FRAME.lo) / BIN));

/*
 * The round ticks, solved once for the shared frame and used by both strips.
 *
 * They used to be solved per strip and then filtered against that strip's own
 * data extremes, so one strip printed 「−5 0」 and the other 「−5 0 +5」 on what
 * is now the same scale — a reader comparing them had to check the numbers
 * before trusting the positions. A tick is dropped only when it would crowd an
 * extreme label in EITHER strip, so the two axes always carry the same set.
 *
 * 12% of the frame, and the two values it was not are worth recording. The last
 * label is pulled fully inside the box, so it reaches further left than its own
 * position says: at 8% 「5」 and 「8.0」 came out 0.7px apart at a 480 viewport and
 * 1.2px OVERLAPPING at 460, which reads as one number. The gap that matters is
 * 10.05% of the frame, so 10% missed it by five hundredths of a point. 12%.
 */
const MARK_CLEARANCE = 0.12;
const MARKS = ticks(FRAME.lo, FRAME.hi, 4)
  .filter((t) => t >= FRAME.lo && t <= FRAME.hi)
  .filter((t) => {
    const at = (t - FRAME.lo) / (FRAME.hi - FRAME.lo);
    return windowed.every((e) => {
      const vals = e.placebo_effects.concat(e.station_effects.map((s) => s.effect));
      return [Math.min(...vals), Math.max(...vals)].every(
        (v) => Math.abs(at - (v - FRAME.lo) / (FRAME.hi - FRAME.lo)) > MARK_CLEARANCE,
      );
    });
  });

function strip(e: (typeof events)[number]) {
  const pool = e.placebo_effects.length ? e.placebo_effects : [];
  const values = pool.concat(e.station_effects.map((s) => s.effect));
  const x = linear([FRAME.lo, FRAME.hi], [0, 100]);
  /*
   * `lo`/`hi` are the frame; `dataLo`/`dataHi` are the data.
   *
   * The axis used to print the frame — `n(s.lo, 1)` where `lo` already had the
   * 5% pad added — so two of its three numbers were layout coordinates wearing
   * the costume of measurements, and they missed the real extremes by 0.97 and
   * 1.31 μg/m³. This chapter's entire subject is effects of about that size. A
   * number on this site is a measurement; a frame edge is not one, and it does
   * not get to be printed as though it were.
   */
  /*
   * Bin the real estimates so coincident stations stack instead of overlapping.
   * The bin is a fixed slice of the FRAME, so a dot's column position is still
   * its value and two stations in the same column are within one bin of each
   * other. Sorted inside the bin so the stack is deterministic between builds.
   */
  const frameLo = FRAME.lo;
  const frameHi = FRAME.hi;
  const width = (frameHi - frameLo) / BINS;
  const bins = new Map<number, { station: string; effect: number }[]>();
  for (const st of [...e.station_effects].sort((a, b) => a.effect - b.effect)) {
    const k = Math.min(BINS - 1, Math.floor((st.effect - frameLo) / width));
    const got = bins.get(k);
    if (got) got.push(st);
    else bins.set(k, [st]);
  }
  const stacked = [...bins.entries()].flatMap(([k, members]) =>
    members.map((st, row) => ({
      ...st,
      // The bin's centre, not the station's own value: a stacked dot plot says
      // "this many are about here", and letting each dot keep its exact x would
      // make a column that leans.
      at: x(frameLo + (k + 0.5) * width),
      row,
    })),
  );
  const tallest = Math.max(1, ...[...bins.values()].map((m) => m.length));

  /*
   * Round tick values across the frame.
   *
   * The axis printed three numbers — the two data extremes and 0 — with no tick
   * marks and no round values between them. Measured at 1440 that is -11.4 at
   * x = 471.6, 0 at 957 and 8.0 ending at 1295: nothing lets a reader locate the
   * −0.96 the prose quotes, or see where the 2.5–3.5 μg/m³ noise floor it names
   * actually falls. Both strips carry a scale a reader can measure against now,
   * and it is the same scale with the same ticks — see `FRAME` and `MARKS`.
   */
  return {
    x,
    marks: MARKS,
    lo: frameLo,
    hi: frameHi,
    dataLo: Math.min(...values),
    dataHi: Math.max(...values),
    pool,
    stacked,
    tallest,
  };
}

const shortName = (name: string) =>
  name.replace("COVID-19 ", "").replace("2、3 號機生煤許可爭議", "生煤許可").replace("2018 ", "");
---

<section id="detection" class="page">
  <ChapterIntro slug="detection">
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>分析方法也有偵測極限。</strong>這一章量的就是它。
  </p>
  </ChapterIntro>

  <EvidenceFigure number="5.1" title="三個事件各自需要多大的效應才看得見？" primary>
  {
    windowed.map((e) => {
      const s = strip(e);
      const zero = s.x(0);
      // The opening strip is the chapter's primary data rectangle, so it keeps
      // the specified 180px of inspectable marks; later strips retain the
      // denser height that lets their repeated comparison stay scannable.
      const primaryStrip = e === windowed[0];
      return (
        <>
        <div class="event-strip-panel">
          <div class="chart">
            {/*
              Named, and deliberately NOT focusable.

              These two were the only plots on the site the accessibility tree
              had nothing to say about: measured across five chapters, ten of
              the twelve `.plot` elements are `tabindex="0"`, `role="group"`,
              with a label ending 「用左右方向鍵逐點讀值」 and a live region —
              and these two were `tabIndex -1`, `role` null, `aria-label` null,
              i.e. `role=generic name=""`. Their 145 marks carry their values in
              `title`, which is a pointer-only channel.

              `role="img"` rather than the `group` the others use, because the
              others earned that role by being operable: they have arrow-key
              traversal and this figure cannot. Its marks are stacked in ROWS by
              bin, not laid along a single axis, so stepping through them would
              need an ordering invented for the purpose — and stepping a screen
              reader through 73 values one press at a time is a poor way to read
              a distribution even where the ordering is obvious. A focus stop
              that does nothing is the defect this project removed from the rail
              checkbox; it does not get reintroduced here for symmetry.

              What the reader needs instead is the numbers, and those are in the
              table below the figure.
            */}
            <div
              class="plot strip"
              role="img"
              aria-label={`${shortName(e.event)}：${e.n_stations} 個測站的估計值，落在 ${s.pool.length} 個安慰劑估計值構成的散布中。逐站數值列在圖下方的表格。`}
              style={`--plot-h:${Math.max(primaryStrip ? 180 : 96, 46 + s.tallest * DOT)}px`}
            >
              <div class="plot-area" data-primary-plot={primaryStrip || undefined}>
                <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
                  {/*
                    One `<path>` per placebo estimate, and the reason it is not
                    ONE path for all of them is the encoding.

                    Each mark is drawn at `opacity: 0.28`, so where estimates
                    coincide the marks composite and darken — that accumulation
                    IS the distribution's shape. Measured on the COVID strip: the
                    densest 0.25-unit bucket holds 20 marks, which composite to
                    an effective alpha of 0.999. Merged into a single path the
                    same peak would render at 0.28, a uniform wash where the
                    argument of the figure is precisely that the estimates pile
                    up around zero. So they stay separate elements.

                    What was wasteful was the MARKUP, not the marks. Each was a
                    `<line>` carrying x twice, y twice, a class and a
                    vector-effect attribute — 150 characters for a 1px hairline,
                    and the coordinates were emitted at 13 decimal places while
                    one pixel is 0.124 viewBox units. A path states x once, the
                    class and the vector-effect move to the parent, and the
                    coordinate is rounded to the last digit that can change a
                    pixel. 150 characters becomes 29.
                  */}
                  {/* Gridlines first, so every mark on the strip sits on top of
                      the scale rather than under it. */}
                  <g class="strip-grid">
                    {s.marks.map((t) => (
                      <path d={`M${n(s.x(t), 2)} 0V100`} vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
                    ))}
                  </g>

                  <g class="placebo-pool">
                    {s.pool.map((v) => <path d={`M${n(s.x(v), 2)} 2V30`} />)}
                  </g>
                  {/*
                    Zero is drawn LAST, and that is the whole point of it.
                    
                    Emitted before the rug, it was painted first and then covered:
                    measured at 1440 on the COVID strip, the 1,295 placebo
                    hairlines cover y 2.9 to 43.2 and the tallest dot column lands
                    within 34px of the rule, so what survived was a 2.9px stub
                    above the band. The figure asks the reader to judge how far
                    the real estimates sit from zero, and zero was the one mark
                    not on screen. Painting it over the cloud costs the cloud one
                    hairline's width and gives the figure its reference back.
                  */}
                  <line x1={zero} x2={zero} y1="0" y2="100" class="zero" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
                </svg>
                {s.stacked.map((st) => (
                  <span
                    class="plot-pt real"
                    style={`left:${n(st.at, 3)}%;top:calc(100% - ${st.row * DOT + 5}px)`}
                    title={`${st.station}: ${n(st.effect, 2)} μg/m³`}
                  />
                ))}
              </div>

              {/* Each label sits at the position of the value it names, which
                  is why they are no longer pinned to 0% and 100%. */}
              <div class="plot-x keep">
                {/* `toFixed`, not `n()`: `n()` drops a trailing zero, so the two
                    ends of the same axis printed 「-11.4」 and 「8」 — one decimal
                    on the left and none on the right, on a pair a reader
                    compares. Both ends say the same precision. */}
                <span style={`left:${n(s.x(s.dataLo), 3)}%`}>{s.dataLo.toFixed(1)}</span>
                {/* The round values between the ends, so a reader can place a
                    number rather than only the extremes. Zero keeps its own
                    span below — it is the reference, not a tick. */}
                {s.marks
                  .filter((t) => t !== 0)
                  .map((t) => (
                    <span class="strip-tick" style={`left:${n(s.x(t), 3)}%`}>{t}</span>
                  ))}
                <span style={`left:${n(zero, 3)}%`}>0</span>
                <span style={`left:${n(s.x(s.dataHi), 3)}%`}>{s.dataHi.toFixed(1)}</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        </>
      );
    })
  }
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    {
      windowed.map((e) => {
        const s = strip(e);
        return (
          <>
          {/* `n_stations` and `station_effects.length` are the same number and
              the caption printed both of them — 「73 站的估計值（73 個亮點）」.
              One of them is the count and the other is how the count is drawn,
              so the second says only what a reader can already see. */}
          <span class="event-caption">
            <strong>{shortName(e.event)}</strong> —— {e.n_stations} 站的估計值
            （亮點）落在 {s.pool.length} 個安慰劑估計值（淡線）構成的散布之中。
            單位 μg/m³，兩張圖共用刻度。
          </span>
          </>
        );
      })
    }
  </Fragment>
  </EvidenceFigure>

  <p>
    這份資料裡有一個 <code>ND</code> 旗標，意思是「低於儀器偵測極限」——
    測不到，不代表沒有，只代表低於這台儀器能分辨的下限。
  </p>

  <p>
    測了三個事件：2018 年空氣污染防制法修正、台中電廠 2、3 號機的生煤許可爭議、
    以及 2021 年的 COVID-19 三級警戒。做法是<strong>反事實</strong>——
    用事件視窗以外的日子訓練模型，再用視窗當時的<strong>實際天氣</strong>去預測，
    觀測值與預測值的差額就是天氣與季節解釋不掉的部分。
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>然後，同一套程序再跑一次沒有事件的年份。</strong>
    同樣的日曆窗口、同樣的模型、同樣的計算——只是那些年什麼都沒發生。
    這叫安慰劑檢定，它問的是：這個方法在應該找不到東西的時候，會找到多少？
  </div>

  <h2>結果</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="dense">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>事件</th>
          <th class="num">測站</th>
          <th class="num">效應中位數</th>
          <th class="num">安慰劑均值</th>
          <th class="num">安慰劑 SD</th>
          <th class="num">通過門檻</th>
          <th class="num">機率預期</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          events.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{shortName(e.event)}</td>
              <td class="num">{e.n_stations}</td>
              <td class="num">{n(e.median_effect, 2)}</td>
              <td class="num dim">{n(e.median_placebo_mean, 2)}</td>
              <td class="num dim">{n(e.median_placebo_sd, 2)}</td>
              <td class="num strong">{e.n_credible}</td>
              <td class="num dim">~{n(e.n_expected_by_chance, 1)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="small">
    「通過門檻」是效應距離該站自己的安慰劑均值 2 個標準差以上的測站數。
    「機率預期」是純粹靠運氣就會通過的站數（2 個標準差在常態分布下約留 4.55% 在雙尾）。
    <strong>三個事件的實際通過數都低於機率預期。</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>估計值與其安慰劑分布的對照</h2>

  <p>
    下面每一條帶狀圖是一個<strong>日曆窗口</strong>事件，所以是兩張，不是三張：
    空污法修正是制度轉變而不是有前後的視窗，它的檢定在本章最後一節，用的是分段迴歸。
    <span class="k-pl">淡色的短線</span>是安慰劑——
    同一個方法在沒有事件的年份算出來的「效應」。
    <span class="k-real">亮色的點</span>是各測站的實際估計值。
    兩張圖共用同一個刻度，零在正中間，所以雲的寬度可以直接比。
  </p>

  {
    windowed.map((e) => (
      <>
        {/*
          The 145 values, in a form that is not a hover.

          Eight stations are named in the prose of this chapter and the other
          137 existed only as `title` attributes on 7×7px marks. That is the
          whole of the figure's data behind a pointer.

          A `<details>`, because the figure is the argument — the estimates sit
          inside the placebo cloud — and the per-station numbers are the
          evidence behind it rather than the point of it. The chapter already
          uses this form for its own detail, the print sheet opens every one,
          and `TableTools` picks the table up by shape: the `<summary>` becomes
          its accessible name and the box becomes keyboard-scrollable at the
          widths where it overflows.

          Sorted by effect, not by station name. The reader's question here is
          which stations moved most, and a table sorted the way the strip is
          drawn can be read against it.
        */}
        <details class="strip-values">
          <summary>{shortName(e.event)}：{e.n_stations} 個測站的逐站估計值</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="dense">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">測站</th>
                  <th scope="col">效應（μg/m³）</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {[...e.station_effects]
                  .sort((a, b) => a.effect - b.effect)
                  .map((st) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{st.station}</td>
                      <td class="num">{st.effect.toFixed(2)}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </details>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <div class="note caveat">
    <strong>安慰劑的均值幾乎從不是零，這件事本身就是重點。</strong>
    COVID 那個日曆窗口的安慰劑均值是 {n(windowed[0]?.median_placebo_mean ?? 0, 2)} μg/m³——
    模型在五到七月系統性高估。所以看到某站「下降 0.96」時，
    真正該問的是：這個方法在沒有封城的年份，平均也會給出多少？
    答案是 {n(windowed[0]?.median_placebo_mean ?? 0, 2)}。
  </div>

  <h2>都會測站：百分比變化與標準化效應量</h2>

  <p>
    封城期間的都會測站：前金 −23.4%、左營 −18.5%、古亭 −9.9%。
    這些數字放進新聞標題毫無問題。它們對應的 z 分數是
    <strong>−0.24、−0.52、−0.12</strong>——全部深深落在安慰劑散布之內。
  </p>

  <h2>空間檢定</h2>

  <p>{spatial.why}</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>測站</th>
          <th class="num">效應 μg/m³</th>
          <th class="num">z</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          spatial.stations.map((s) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{s.station}</td>
              <td class={`num ${s.effect > 0 ? "worse" : ""}`}>{n(s.effect, 2)}</td>
              <td class="num dim">{n(s.z, 2)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="small">
    而三個事件裡唯一通過門檻的測站，都不在該有效應的地方：
    COVID 是潮州（屏東鄉村，但封城效應該在交通主導的都會站最明顯）；
    台中電廠是麥寮（雲林的石化園區，離台中電廠約 50 公里的另一個設施）。
  </p>

  {
    breaks.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2>制度轉變：分段趨勢的斜率檢定</h2>
        <p>
          一條法規生效之後就一直生效，它不是一個有前後的視窗，而是一次制度轉變。
          該改變的是下降的<strong>速率</strong>。所以空污法修正改用分段迴歸，
          跑在<a href={href("trend")}>第一章</a>那條氣象正規化序列的逐月版本上——
          那條線平滑（重抽樣把季節循環一起移除了，所以第一章只畫年度），
          解析不出十週的封城，卻正好適合問一個多年的下降有沒有轉折。
        </p>
        <p class="small">
          結果：{breaks[0].n_stations} 站中 {breaks[0].n_credible} 站通過門檻，
          機率預期約 {n(breaks[0].n_expected_by_chance, 1)} 站。
          斜率變化的中位數是 {n(breaks[0].median_effect, 3)} μg/m³/年²。
          <strong>普遍的減速幾乎確定是邊際報酬遞減</strong>——
          濃度從 30 降到 15 之後，不可能維持同樣的絕對降速。
        </p>
      </>
    )
  }

  <div class="note caveat">
    <strong>「測不到」不等於「等於零」。</strong>
    這個方法在這些日曆窗口的噪音底線是 2.5–3.5 μg/m³，
    而待測的效應量是 0.5–1.6 μg/m³。<strong>噪音底線高於訊號。</strong>
    真正的結論是：<strong>這批資料與這個方法，無法分辨這種大小的效應</strong>——
    不是「這些事件沒有影響」。
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>台中電廠這一筆需要補充說明。</strong>
    市府的處分後來被環保署撤銷，台電當時聲明將提行政救濟，
    而機組是否真的降載並沒有公開紀錄——周邊 8 站也確實毫無反應。
    最合理的解讀是<strong>機組從未真的停止燃煤</strong>。
    也就是說，這裡的「測不到」比較可能是「事情沒發生」，而不是「事情發生了但沒效果」。
    這兩件事必須分開。
  </div>
</section>

